  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ツノナス」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%84%E3%83%8E%E3%83%8A%E3%82%B9",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 111211913,
      "text": "ツノナス は、ブラジル原産のナス科の独特な形の果実をつける植物である。一年生植物との表記が多いが日本などの高緯度の国々でのことであり、原産地の低緯度地域では実際は冬を越す。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Solanum mammosum」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solanum_mammosum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1373354373,
      "text": "Solanum mammosum, commonly known as nipplefruit, fox head, cow's udder, or apple of Sodom, is an inedible Pan-American tropical fruit. The plant is grown for ornamental purposes, in part because of the distal end of the fruit's resemblance to a human breast, while the proximal end looks like a cow's udder. It is an annual in the family Solanaceae, and part of the genus Solanum, making the plant a relative of the eggplant, tomato, and potato. This poisonous fruit is native to South America, but has been naturalized in Southern Mexico, Greater Antilles, Central America, and the Caribbean. The plant adapts well to most soils, but thrives in moist, loamy soil."
    }
  },
